# Website Technology Stack for Claude Code Driven Development (2026/2027)

## Purpose

This document defines the recommended technology stack for building and running a business website where Claude Code drives development and the application lifecycle. The selection principle is simple: choose the stack that gives the agent the fastest and most reliable feedback, not the most fashionable one.

## Selection Principles

1. **Popular and well documented.** Frameworks with large public bodies of code produce the highest quality agent output.
2. **Strictly typed and testable.** Types, linters, and automated tests let the agent verify its own work before a human reviews it.
3. **Everything as code.** Configuration, content schemas, infrastructure, and pipelines live in the repository so the agent can read, change, and test them without using a dashboard.
4. **Humans own accountability.** The agent drives the work. The team approves, releases, and answers for it.

## Recommended Stack

### Application

| Layer | Choice | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Framework | Next.js (App Router) | Market default with the deepest ecosystem and strongest agent familiarity |
| Language | TypeScript, strict mode | Catches errors at compile time and gives the agent a verifiable contract |
| Styling | Tailwind CSS | Utility classes keep styling inside the component, easy for the agent to reason about |
| Components | shadcn/ui | Source-owned components that the agent can read and modify directly |

Use Astro instead of Next.js if the site is almost entirely static content with no logins, dashboards, or interactive tools.

### Content

| Scenario | Choice |
|---|---|
| Developers own all content changes | MDX files stored in the repository |
| Marketing or sales staff must edit without developers | Sanity, with schemas defined in code |

Sanity can be added later without a rebuild, so start with MDX if there is doubt.

### Data (only if required)

| Layer | Choice |
|---|---|
| Database | Postgres through Supabase or Neon |
| Data access | Drizzle or Prisma for typed queries |
| Authentication | Auth.js or Clerk, added only when user logins are needed |

### Verification

| Purpose | Tool |
|---|---|
| Unit tests | Vitest |
| End-to-end and visual checks | Playwright |
| Code quality | ESLint and Prettier |
| Type safety | TypeScript compiler in strict mode |

These tools are the guardrails that make autonomous work trustworthy. No change merges unless all of them pass.

### Lifecycle and Operations

| Purpose | Tool |
|---|---|
| Source control | GitHub |
| Continuous integration | GitHub Actions |
| Hosting and deployment | Vercel, with preview deployments on every pull request |
| Error monitoring | Sentry |
| Performance | Vercel Analytics for Core Web Vitals |
| Search and traffic | Google Search Console and Plausible or GA4 |
| DNS and edge security | Cloudflare |

## Agent Configuration

- **CLAUDE.md** at the repository root, describing folder structure, naming conventions, testing expectations, and the definition of done.
- **MCP connections** to GitHub and the hosting provider so the agent can open pull requests and inspect deployments.
- **Claude Code in CI** to review pull requests, fix failing checks, and propose dependency updates.
- **Small, scoped tasks.** Each task maps to one branch and one pull request, which keeps review fast and rollback simple.

## Human Control Points

The team retains authority in four places:

1. Pull request approval before any merge to the main branch.
2. Secrets, API keys, and account access.
3. Production release.
4. Security and accessibility sign-off.

## Technical Baseline for Every Page

- Strong Core Web Vitals as a build-blocking budget.
- JSON-LD structured data and a clear content hierarchy for search engines and AI assistants.
- Semantic HTML and sufficient colour contrast.
- HTTPS everywhere and regular dependency updates.
- A privacy notice aligned to POPIA where personal data is collected. Refer interpretation of POPIA obligations to Johan van Zyl, external legal counsel.
- Domain, hosting, CMS, and repository held under company accounts, never personal ones.

## What to Avoid

- Visual page builders that cannot be read or tested by an agent.
- Obscure or very new frameworks with limited public examples.
- Infrastructure configured by hand through a dashboard.
- Secrets stored in the repository or pasted into prompts.

## Adoption Path

1. Scaffold the repository with Next.js, TypeScript, Tailwind, and shadcn/ui.
2. Add linting, type checks, Vitest, and Playwright, then wire them into GitHub Actions.
3. Write CLAUDE.md and connect MCP tools.
4. Connect the repository to Vercel for preview and production deployments.
5. Add Sentry and analytics before the first production release.
6. Introduce Sanity or a database only when a real requirement appears.
